## Core Components

### 1. Agent (`app/main.py`)

Main entry point with two operational modes:

**Foreground Mode** (for development):
```bash
poetry run python -m app.main --foreground --debug
```
- Runs in terminal with logging output
- Uses asyncio event loop on main thread
- Web server and file watcher in background threads
- Ctrl+C to stop

**Menu Bar Mode** (production):
```bash
poetry run python -m app.main
```
- Runs as macOS menu bar app (rumps); asyncio runs in a background thread
- Logs to `~/.config/rmirror/agent.log` (also printed to the terminal in foreground mode)
- Menu bar icon with status, open web UI, check for updates, settings, quit

### 2. File Watcher (`app/watcher/`)

Monitors reMarkable sync folder for changes:
- Uses `watchdog` library
- Detects new/modified/moved `.rm`, `.metadata` and `.content` files
- Triggers upload via CloudSync
- Runs in background thread

### 3. Cloud Sync (`app/sync/`)

Handles HTTP API calls to backend:
The API base is `config.api.url` (default `https://rmirror.io/api/v1`; local backend: `http://localhost:8000/v1`).

```python
# Phase 1: register notebook/page structure (app/sync/metadata_sync.py)
POST {api}/sync/metadata

# Phase 2: upload one page (multipart form, raw .rm file; page_uuid = filename)
POST {api}/processing/rm-file

# Page order for a notebook (.content file)
POST {api}/notebooks/{notebook_uuid}/content

# Notebook metadata (.metadata file)
POST {api}/processing/metadata/update

# Status: GET /sync/stats, GET /quota/status, GET /users/me,
# GET /sync/deleted-notebooks, POST /sync/deleted-notebooks/{uuid}/acknowledge
```

### 4. Web UI (`app/web/`)

Flask web server for configuration:
- Runs on `localhost:5555`
- Shows sync status, settings
- Clerk sign-in flow (`/auth/bridge`, `/auth/callback`) for backend authentication
- Runs in background thread (non-blocking)

### 5. Menu Bar App (`app/tray/`)

macOS menu bar integration using `rumps`:
- Shows sync status icon
- Menu items: Status, Open Web UI, Check for Updates, Settings, Quit
- Must run on main thread (macOS AppKit requirement)

## Configuration

Configuration is read from the first existing of `~/.config/rmirror/config.yaml`, `~/.rmirror/config.yaml`, `./config.yaml` (override with `--config` or `RMIRROR_CONFIG_PATH`; `Config.save()` writes `~/.config/rmirror/config.yaml`, mode 0600). Settings can also come from `RMIRROR_*` environment variables. Models: `app/config.py`.

```yaml
# Backend API
api:
  url: "https://rmirror.io/api/v1"  # local backend: "http://localhost:8000/v1"

# reMarkable Desktop sync folder (this is the default)
remarkable:
  source_directory: "~/Library/Containers/com.remarkable.desktop/Data/Library/Application Support/remarkable/desktop"
  watch_enabled: true

# Web UI
web:
  enabled: true
  host: "127.0.0.1"
  port: 5555

# Sync behaviour (see SyncConfig in app/config.py)
sync:
  auto_sync: true
  max_pages_per_notebook: null   # null = unlimited, otherwise newest pages first
```

The debug flag (`--debug`) sets the log level; there is no `logging:` section.

## Authentication Flow

The agent uses long-lived JWT tokens (30 days) obtained via Clerk OAuth:

1. User clicks "Sign in" in web UI
2. With a production Clerk key (`pk_live_...`) the agent redirects to `{site}/agent?callback=http://localhost:{PORT}/auth/callback` (dashboard page `dashboard/app/agent/page.tsx`); with a dev key it serves its own `app/web/templates/auth_bridge.html` at `/auth/bridge`
3. User authenticates with Clerk (Google OAuth)
4. Bridge page exchanges Clerk session token for 30-day agent JWT (`POST /auth/agent-token`)
5. Bridge page redirects to agent callback with token
6. Agent stores token in system keychain (secure)
7. Token included in all API requests

**Token Details**:
- Expiration: 30 days (vs ~1 hour for Clerk session tokens)
- Type: Custom JWT with `type: "agent"` claim
- Storage: macOS Keychain via `keyring` library
- Refresh: none; when the token expires (HTTP 401) the user signs in again via the web UI

```python
# app/auth/keychain.py: service "com.rmirror.agent", account = API hostname
# (so production and staging tokens do not overwrite each other)
config.api.token = token      # setter stores it in the keychain
token = config.api.token      # getter reads it back

# Use in API calls (CloudSync._get_headers)
headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}"}
```

